By [[Ellen Lupton]] and J. Abbott Miller. First printed as "Deconstruction and Graphic Design: History Meets Theory" in //Visible Language// 28, no. 4 (Fall 1994). [[Andrew Blauvelt]] edited that issue, a special issue on critical histories of graphic design. A revised version is in their book //Design Writing Research//.

Their claim is that deconstruction is not a style. It is a way of questioning: the tools, the formal devices, the institutions, and the founding metaphors that representation is built on.

It belongs to the second of Blauvelt's three waves, content. See [[Towards Relational Design]].

